<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arthashastra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Arthashastra is an ancient Indian treatise on statecraft, economics and military strategy, traditionally attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanakya">Kautilya</a> (Chanakya), adviser to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a>. It describes administration, taxation, espionage and diplomacy in detail. Although its date and composition are debated, it is widely used to understand the governance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> title can be rendered as a treatise on political science, economic science or simply statecraft, since the word artha has a broad range of meanings. Artha, prosperity or material security, is one of the four aims of human life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a>, kama and moksha. The text names two compilers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanakya">Kautilya</a> (or Kautalya) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnugupta">Vishnugupta</a>, and a later interpolation and the traditions of the Gupta era and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> identified Kautilya with Chanakya. Scholarship has questioned that identification, and the main studies since 1965 reject it.</p><p> Patrick Olivelle and McClish hold that the work grew in layers: earlier arthashastras dating from the 2nd century BCE to the 1st century CE were compiled in the 1st century CE, and a major redaction followed in the 2nd or 3rd century CE. Stylistic differences point to several hands, and some details fit the first centuries CE better than the Mauryan age.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The surviving text has 15 books, 150 chapters and 180 topics. It covers the training of a king, the work of the superintendents who ran mines, workshops, customs, agriculture and shipping, civil and criminal law, spying, diplomacy and war. It also deals with ministers, marriage and the remarriage of women, forests and wildlife, and taxation. The economy it describes is mixed: mines were owned by the state but leased to private parties, and private enterprise was taxed and regulated.</p><p>The ideal ruler is the sage king, who masters lust, anger, greed, conceit, arrogance and foolhardiness. On war, the text advises that where progress is equal, peace is to be preferred, since war brings losses, expense and absence from home. Books on foreign policy set out the sixfold strategy and the theory of the circle of states, and the text recommends a network of trained spies, including the use of women to sow quarrels among the chiefs of an enemy council.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The work was influential until the 12th century, when it disappeared from view. R. Shamasastry rediscovered a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> in 1905, published the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> text in 1909 and the first English translation in 1915. Verses of the work have also been found on fragments from Buddhist monasteries in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, among them the Spitzer Manuscript, of about 200 CE.</p><p>In 1919 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-weber">Max Weber</a> wrote that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Machiavelli</a>'s The Prince was harmless beside it. Later readers disagree about its character: Roger Boesche compared its state to a welfare-minded surveillance state, while Thomas Trautmann stresses that it recognises private property and land sales, though under rules of first refusal that do not make a free market.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arthashastra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Arthashastra</b>, attributed to Chanakya but probably compiled and revised over several centuries, gives detailed guidance on administration, espionage, law and diplomacy, and is often used to illuminate Mauryan practice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…of myth and history; the treatises on law and statecraft, such as the Laws of Manu and the <b>Arthashastra</b>; the Kamasutra; the theory of drama and aesthetic emotion of the Natyashastra; and the animal fables of the Panchatantra, which travelled through Middle Persian and Arabic, as Kalila and…</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Megasthenes described a hereditary class of soldiers, the second most numerous, paid by the state and idle in peacetime, but the <b>Arthashastra</b> expected troops of every varna.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classical-india">Social Hierarchy of Classical India</a></li><li><blockquote>Kautilya's <b>Arthashastra</b> describes the Kambojas as a sangha whose members lived by trade and arms, suggesting rule by a warrior aristocracy rather than a king.</blockquote><a href="/state/kamboja">Kamboja</a></li></ul></section>
